WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:06.350
Some of you may wake up in the morning and
think; “Me think nice, me big smart, me

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:09.410
no need school”
And perhaps you are right.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:15.530
But many of us, no matter what qualifications
we have or what work we do, often wonder;

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:19.510
is my brain achieving all it could achieve?

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:23.240
Maybe it’s as simple as doing a bit more
exercise, eating more fish and not drinking

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:26.260
like the world’s going to end 2 nights a
week.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:31.429
But are there other factors that affect how
well your brain does its job?

00:00:31.429 --> 00:00:36.270
Does knowledge itself make you smarter or
is it just data that you spit out randomly?

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:41.000
What about puzzles and games, do these help
at all after childhood or are they simply

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.140
an escape?

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:47.560
And how about surgeries or drugs that could
give you some kind of edge on the competition?

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:51.880
So watch out Steven Hawking, I’m going to
climb that IQ ladder and kick you off the

00:00:51.880 --> 00:01:00.079
top once I find out: Can I Get Smarter?

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:04.739
First off let’s all discuss the idea of
intelligence, the way it’s defined and the

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:05.780
way it’s measured.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:11.570
On a physical level, we know relatively little
about what makes one brain intelligent compared

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:12.570
to another.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:18.300
There’s no one bit of structure or biochemistry
that we can point to and go “ah ha, there’s

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:19.560
the smart bit”.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.860
Here’s what we do know; the brain is made
up of hundreds of billions of cells and different

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:31.750
sections are responsible for different functions
such as language, movement and… um……

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:33.040
memory.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:38.130
Information is passed around the brain by
neurons and synapses, where neurons are electrically

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:42.780
excitable cells and synapses are the connections
between them.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:49.020
When you are developing, these grow at an
astonishing rate; you create 1,300 neurons

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.070
and 700,000 synapses every second.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:58.970
This is your DNA, rapidly coding that giant
computer brain.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:04.120
Scientists actually dissected Einstein’s
brain, after he died obviously, a bit awkward

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:05.120
otherwise.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:10.170
One of the structural differences they found
was that he had a noticeably higher ratio

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:15.810
of glia to neurons, with glia being a different
kind of brain cell.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:21.960
This indicates that there may be some quantifiable
difference in the brains of smart people,

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.150
but we still don’t know for sure.

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:30.100
So for now, we focus much more on ability
tests, rather than the brain’s physical

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.770
structure, to measure intelligence.

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:37.530
Francis Galton was an English statistician
and the first person to attempt to develop

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:41.950
some kind of standardised test for gauging
a person’s intelligence.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:48.200
He expected to find a correlation between
intellect and other factors such as reflexes,

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.600
muscle grip, and head size.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:56.010
If this were true, I think LL Cool J would
probably be the world’s smartest man as

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.350
he’s basically a watermelon with bear paws.

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:06.370
In the 1940s, psychologist Raymond Cattell
laid out the idea of two main types of intelligence;

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:08.470
fluid and crystalized.

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:14.190
Fluid was the ability to react to new situations
and solve novel problems.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:18.420
Crystalized intelligence involves using skills
and knowledge that you already have.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:24.140
It’s not the same as memory but it will
rely on long-term memory, especially for things

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:26.550
like vocabulary and general knowledge.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:32.440
In the following years, John L. Horn and John
B. Carroll added further elements to Cattell’s

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:39.820
ideas and the Cattell–Horn–Carroll theory
is the basis of most current IQ testing.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:46.790
The average IQ is 100, with the huge majority
of people sitting between 70 and 130.

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:53.350
A score above 130 is considered gifted and
above 144 is genius level.

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:59.630
William James Sidis was supposed to have had
the highest IQ of all time with a possible

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:01.880
number of 300.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:07.110
He entered Harvard for mathematics in 1910,
at just 11 years old.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:10.610
Now, where does smartness come from?

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:14.680
This is the big Nature versus Nurture question.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:19.250
If you take the child of two knuckle dragging
morons and give it to some fancypants, genius

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:25.410
academics, will the child grow into a specky
little brainbox or a crayon-chewing bonehead?

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:30.880
We used to think that the balance was more
even between the two factors but as more evidence

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:37.461
appears, we see that genetics is by far the
most important factor in how smart a child

00:04:37.461 --> 00:04:38.530
can become.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:44.100
Robert Plomin, working at King’s College
London, ran the Colorado Adoption Project,

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.770
which began back in 1975.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:52.710
For over 30 years they tracked adopted children,
their adoptive parents and their biological

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:57.800
parents to see where the most influence came
from.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:02.100
They also used control families, where the
kids were raised normally by their biological

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:03.100
parents.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:08.470
In the control groups, they found a strong
correlation between the parent’s and children’s

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:14.139
IQs, so this could have been all nature, all
nurture or a balance of the two.

00:05:14.139 --> 00:05:19.970
But with the adopted children there is exactly
the same correlation to their biological parents

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:26.180
and ZERO correlation of their intelligence
with their adoptive parents, the people who

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:28.020
actually raised them.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:35.330
So smart adoptive parents are not able to
raise a smart adopted child if the biological

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:40.389
parents of that child aren’t smart to begin
with, no matter what the adoptive parents

00:05:40.389 --> 00:05:41.389
do.

00:05:41.389 --> 00:05:45.440
You can’t teach an old dog new tricks, or
a stupid kid how to play the piano.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:50.840
What’s more, it seems that most of your
personality is also genetic, so things like

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:56.210
being introverted and extroverted come from
your DNA, not from your home environment.

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:59.440
So is nurture completely irrelevant?

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:05.900
No, our environment still has a big influence
on us, especially in terms of behavior, but

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:11.510
psychologist Judith Rich Harris believes that
we have been focusing on the wrong part of

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:12.510
it.

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:17.500
The purpose of childhood is to prepare you
to be an adult, and being an adult does not

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:19.830
require you to get on with your parents.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:24.510
Obviously, it’s preferable, so please don’t
finish this video and go shout “I don’t

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:25.510
need you anymore!”

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:28.720
whilst your mum is making dinner, because
you won’t get any.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:32.340
It’s your peers that influence your behavior
the most.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:37.210
That’s why the children of immigrants will
speak like the local population, not like

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:38.470
Mum and Dad.

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:43.680
Habits like smoking and drinking are normally
picked up from peers but a disposition to

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:49.060
addiction is passed down genetically, which
is why parents who smoke often have children

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:50.690
who smoke too.

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:55.450
They are not copying their parents, they usually
start smoking with their peers and then the

00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:59.100
genes kick in that makes them hopelessly addicted.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:03.780
If you want to try and give your brain a boost,
you can’t mess with your genetics, so I’m

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:09.630
sorry to say, you’re kind of limited to
what you were given, but you can improve your

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:15.440
crystalized intelligence and a thing called
your working memory, which helps your fluid

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:17.960
intelligence to function as well as possible.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:23.270
Much like the gym, the more effort you put
in, the better results you get out; so slip

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:27.240
your brain into some spandex and make sure
you work it out every day.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:31.240
You should challenge yourself as often as
you can and try to do things the hard way,

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:35.370
like working out your own route on a map,
rather than just following that blue arrow

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:38.100
on your satnav like it’s your guardian angel.

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:42.430
Although, please don’t write me an angry
comment from a car park 3 miles away because

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:44.070
you got lost trying to buy milk.

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:47.980
It’s not my fault you have no sense of direction.

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:50.259
Novelty is an important factor too.

00:07:50.259 --> 00:07:54.830
If you always watch the same show, sit in
the same chair and eat the same dinner, then

00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:59.009
not only do you really need to clean your
sofa but your brain is not getting the new

00:07:59.009 --> 00:08:01.960
inputs it needs to learn more.

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:06.560
Try taking a different route to school or
work, try and learn a new skill like origami

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:09.940
or knitting or... whatever the hell this is.

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:15.980
The easiest way to get novelty is by networking,
either in the real world or online.

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:21.590
Other people’s ideas may often be weird,
terrifying and confusing but they will enable

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:23.810
you to build new ones of your own.

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:29.550
It’s no coincidence that over the centuries,
major jumps in science and technology occur

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:32.919
as we improve communication.

00:08:32.919 --> 00:08:38.319
All ideas are built off other ideas so try
to steal as many as possible from other people

00:08:38.319 --> 00:08:41.789
and become the super villain you were destined
to be.

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:47.730
Finally, for those of you who find learning
a struggle, there is light at the end of the

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:52.009
tunnel, but that maybe because they just turned
up the voltage too high and you’re being

00:08:52.009 --> 00:08:53.009
electrocuted.

00:08:53.009 --> 00:08:58.279
That’s right, a study from 2013 found that
stimulating the brain with a mild electric

00:08:58.279 --> 00:09:05.040
current, could lead to people learning five
times faster than normal, and with good retention

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:08.290
six months later, not just in the immediate
aftermath.

00:09:08.290 --> 00:09:12.749
If any of you are thinking of licking the
car battery before doing your homework, don’t.

00:09:12.749 --> 00:09:16.910
If you really think it’s a good idea to
go out of your way to electrocute your brain,

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:20.970
then passing that maths test is the least
of your worries, you should probably sit in

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:23.050
the corner and stay away from sharp objects.

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:44.480
I’m sorry to tell you, but 
your parents were not intelligent.

